A||||8||Child Of Rod||Starting Fiero...sitting in weeds for 10 years.||||5||
Z||000000||Child Of Rod||04-26-2000||12:30 PM||||Well I am picking up my Fieros tomorrow.  <BR>One of them has a bad engine, the other hasn't been started in 10 years.<BR>Both are automatics, with ac.<BR>Is there anything special I should do before I just crank the engine to see if I can get it running?<BR>Should I change the oil?<BR>Flush the radiator?<BR>How do I flush the tranny fluid?<BR>Anything with the brake fluid?<BR>And should I drain the gas tank?(how?)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||04-26-2000||12:55 PM||||Good luck with your new fieros.<P>On the one that has not been started in 10 years, replace the plugs, wires, distributor cap, rotor, fuel filter, air cleaner, and pcv valve.<P>drop the gas tank, throw out the sludge, clean out the tank, put a new fuel pump and sump screen back in the tank.<P>to drop the tank, raise the car rest it on four jack stands. there are two straps that hold up the tank, you will need a 13mm socket for the nut, disconnected the fuel hoses.  dropping the tank is fairly easy. while the tank is down replace your fuel hoses.<P>check the coolant, and see the condition, but probably would not hurt to change it, however if it is okay, service the coolant after the engine runs.  on the idea of coolant, check your radiator hoses, all four.  also check your heater core hoses.<P>yes, change the oil and filter.||||reg||10
Z||000002||1FieroGTRacingInc||04-26-2000||01:08 PM||||Sound's like fun...  LOL    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000003||Child Of Rod||04-26-2000||02:25 PM||||I actually am really looking foward to it. <BR>I have had to do alot of these things with the classic mustangs I've had so it's all fun to me these day.  <BR>I need something to do anyway, work doesn't start for a month and a half, and I have some money to spend.<BR>Now how long does it take to do the v8 conversion?||||reg||6
Z||000004||lennyb4||04-26-2000||02:45 PM||||If i was you i would also turn the engine a couple of revolutions by hand prior to trying it under power. Just place the correct size socket on the harmonic balancer nut, take a deep breath and turn. Remember to turn it clockwise as not to loosen the nut.||||reg||6
Z||000005||peterh||04-26-2000||03:10 PM||||My recommendation is to put a couple of squirts of thin oil down each spark plug hole to lubricate the rings a little.<BR>Good luck.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||04-26-2000||04:23 PM||||The comment about the gas tank and fuel lines is an EXCELLENT suggestion. I recently read about a Indy that the new owner set on fire because of some rotted fuel lines. So was the part about the oil in the cylindars and the new plugs, cap and rotor.<P>In addition, I would start it like a new motor. Pull the plugs and squirt a couple of shots of motor oil into each cylinder. Leave the plugs out and crank the motor using the starter. If you have an oil pressure gauge, watch to see if the pressure starts to rise. What you want to do is to pre-lube the entire motor. Only crank for about 30 seconds max and alow to cool for a couple of minutes. One to two cycles should be plenty.<P>When you change the oil, be sure to pre-fill the oil filter so the motor doesn't starve for oil before the filter is filled. You may even consider doing the pre-lube of the motor with the old oil. The filter should already be full of oil and the rest of the motor is going to be VERY dry.<P>New plugs are a must. Cap and rotor would also be a good idea since they are probably corroded from moisture and age. New wires at your discression. <P>Buy some starting fluid in a spray can. Double check the spark plug wires are installed correctly.<P>Once everything is pre-lubed, the plugs are gapped and installed, the cap, rotor and wires are in place, and fresh gas in the tank, only then do you try to apply the spark. Use the starting fluid lightly and at arms length.<P>Have fun,<BR>Roy<P>P.S., It's always a good idea to have a fire extinguisher handy for emergencys.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||04-26-2000||05:19 PM||||I'd be worried about not having any seals left after a car has been sitting for 10 years.  If it were me, I don't think I'd even bother trying to start it.  Why was it sitting so long anyways?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Child Of Rod||04-27-2000||12:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GT Bastard:<BR><B>I'd be worried about not having any seals left after a car has been sitting for 10 years.  If it were me, I don't think I'd even bother trying to start it.  Why was it sitting so long anyways?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was sitting in the back of this old lady's house,  I have seen it while growing up, sitting there.<BR>The other day I asked about it, and she told me that her son bought it for her new, and that she drove it for 3 years then parked it in the garage for a few years.  It was then moved outside.  It ran prefect the last time it was moved...but hasn't been run since.<BR>Since it only has 37,000 miles on it...I am really looking foward to getting going.||||reg||1
